---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import DataTable from '../components/DataTable.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="UniDPP is an open, international initiative: the mission, multi-body stewardship across CalConnect, ISO/TC 154, OIML, ELF, Enosema, and the UnitsML coalition, governance, licensing, and how to participate."
  eyebrow="The initiative"
>
  <h1>About the initiative</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    UniDPP is an open, international framework and reference implementation for the
    Digital Product Passport. No single organization owns it: each steward body is
    responsible for the layer it already operates as an international standards
    activity, and the software is developed in the open under permissive licences.
  </p>

  <section aria-labelledby="mission-h">
    <h2 id="mission-h">Mission</h2>
    <p>
      Products cross borders; their passports must not stop at them. The initiative
      exists to specify and implement one neutral international core — one product
      identity, one append-only record — on which every jurisdiction's, sector's, and
      characteristic's requirements operate as independently governed profiles. The
      European Union's EN&nbsp;18216–18246 series is treated as exactly one profile among
      others, on equal footing; the series' own neutrality language is implemented
      rather than amended.
    </p>
    <p>
      The work has three inseparable halves: a
      <a href="/specs/">specification</a> in five parts, an
      <a href="/implementation/">open reference implementation</a> with published
      conformance suites, and <a href="/papers/">standards contributions</a> carried to
      ISO/IEC JTC&nbsp;5 and partner bodies through national member bodies and liaison
      channels. The project contributes implementation experience and datasets; it does
      not create a rival standards track.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="stewardship" class="plate" aria-labelledby="stewardship-h">
    <h2 id="stewardship-h">Stewardship</h2>
    <p>
      The framework is multi-body by design: the bodies that already operate each layer
      as an international standards activity steward that layer here.
    </p>
    <DataTable
      columns={['Body', 'Responsibility']}
      rows={[
        ['<strong>CalConnect</strong>', 'Project home; terminology; time, events, and the state machine; offline freshness; SIGNATIF with TC 154; the UnitsML quantity chain.'],
        ['<strong>ISO/TC 154</strong>', 'Identity; trade-documented seams; registry governance with UN/CEFACT; issuance rules; the NWIP vehicle.'],
        ['<strong>OIML</strong>', 'Measurement validity and decision rules; CNML/ESDC; issuance-role governance; the first vertical pilot; the twin ladder.'],
        ['<strong>ELF</strong>', 'The EXPRESS core; seam contracts (S3/S8); the mapping technical report; the AP242 identity-lattice lineage; OpenCDD dictionary technology.'],
        ['<strong>Enosema</strong>', 'FERIN registry operations; ISO 19135:2025 expressibility guarantees.'],
        ['<strong>UnitsML coalition</strong>', 'The quantity chain: ISO 80000 → UnitsDB/UnitsML → OpenCDD.'],
      ]}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="governance" class="plate" aria-labelledby="governance-h">
    <h2 id="governance-h">Governance and licensing</h2>
    <p>
      The standards work is carried in ISO/IEC JTC&nbsp;5 and partner bodies — ISO/TC 154,
      OIML, UN/CEFACT, and CEN where the European profile requires it — and is
      contributed through national member bodies and liaison channels.
    </p>
    <p>
      The software is licensed Apache-2.0 or MIT per repository; the specification is
      CC-BY-4.0; the website and the terminology dataset are MIT. There is no closed
      core and no boundary package around a private backend: every algorithm the
      framework specifies is published with a conformance suite.
    </p>
    <p>
      Guardrails the project holds to: the EN series' own neutrality language is
      enforced rather than amended; no rival central registry — the multi-log quorum
      answers “who runs it”; the terminology absorbs no AAS vocabulary outside an
      informative annex; and internal discipline first — one canonical units chain,
      one registry, one trust framework.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="participate" class="plate" aria-labelledby="participate-h">
    <h2 id="participate-h">How to participate</h2>
    <p>
      Three paths, depending on what you bring:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Implement</strong> — use the reference stack (Rust core, TypeScript,
        Ruby, and Python SDKs), run the conformance suites, and file what you find.
        Start at the <a href="/implementation/">implementation page</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Contribute</strong> — the repositories live at
        <a href="https://github.com/unidpp">github.com/unidpp</a>; corrections to this
        site, the specification snapshot, and the dataset are pull requests. See
        <a href="/community/">community</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Standards participation</strong> — if your organization is a member of
        one of the steward bodies or of ISO/IEC JTC&nbsp;5, the contributions are
        designed to be picked up through your national member body. The catalogue is
        on the <a href="/papers/">papers page</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="contact" aria-labelledby="contact-h">
    <h2 id="contact-h">Contact</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Source: <a href="https://github.com/unidpp">github.com/unidpp</a></li>
      <li>Email: <a href="mailto:contact@unidpp.org">contact@unidpp.org</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
